          id: '101_1_8_2',
          question: 'Analyze the economic life of the Harappan Civilization.',
          answer: 'The Harappan economy was diversified, sophisticated, and commercially oriented—characteristics that set it apart from contemporary Bronze Age civilizations.\n\nAgriculture formed the economic base. The Harappans cultivated wheat, barley, cotton, sesame, rice (at Lothal), dates, and peas in the fertile flood plains of the Indus. The discovery of the world\'s earliest cotton fabric from Mohenjo-daro indicates that cotton cultivation and textile production were established.\n\nCrafts and industry were highly developed. Harappan artisans produced fine wheel-made pottery, bronze tools and weapons, gold and silver jewelry, terracotta figurines, and the famous steatite seals. Specialized crafts such as bead-making using carnelian, lapis lazuli, and faience were practiced at dedicated workshop sites like Chanhu-daro.\n\nTrade was a defining feature. Standardized weights and measures (using a binary and decimal system) facilitated commerce. Long-distance trade with Mesopotamia is well-documented: Harappan seals and beads have been found in Mesopotamian cities, and Mesopotamian texts mention trade with "Meluhha" (identified with the Indus region). The dockyard at Lothal (Gujarat) indicates maritime trade capability. Internal trade routes connected distant parts of the civilization.\n\nThe elaborate standardization of weights, bricks, and seal designs across hundreds of kilometers implies a strong regulatory mechanism—possibly a merchant guild or state apparatus overseeing commercial activity.\n\nThe economic prosperity of the Harappan civilization rested on the combination of agricultural surplus, specialized craft production, and active long-distance trade. This commercial sophistication contributed to the rise and sustenance of the first urban civilization in South Asia.',
