          id: '101_1_8_3',
          question: 'Describe the social organization and religious life of the Early Vedic Aryans.',
          answer: 'The Early Vedic Age (c. 1500–1000 BCE) is documented primarily through the Rigveda, which provides rich insights into the social organization and religious beliefs of the Aryans.\n\nSociety was organized around kinship. The basic unit was the family (kula) under the father (grhapati). Several families formed a grama (village), several villages a vish (clan), and several clans a jana (tribe). Four varnas—Brahmin, Kshatriya, Vaishya, and Shudra—existed but were not rigidly hereditary; a man could change his occupation. Women enjoyed relatively high status: they could study the Vedas, compose hymns (Gargi, Maitreyi), attend assemblies, and remarry.\n\nEarly Vedic religion was nature worship. The Aryans personified natural forces as gods: Indra (thunder and warfare) was the most important deity; Agni (fire) served as intermediary between humans and gods; Varuna embodied cosmic order (Rita); Surya (sun), Usha (dawn), Vayu (wind), and Soma (sacred plant) were other major deities. Religion was conducted through yajna (fire sacrifice), in which offerings of ghee, grain, and soma were poured into fire while Vedic hymns were recited.\n\nThe yajna served multiple purposes—securing divine favor for cattle, children, victory in war, and rain. The Purohita (priest) conducted ritual, and family relationships and tribal bonds defined social identity.\n\nEarly Vedic religion was relatively simple—without elaborate ritual—reflecting a mobile, pastoral people engaged in a direct relationship with nature. This simplicity would change dramatically in the Later Vedic Age, when ritualism became more complex and socially stratified.',
