          id: '101_1_8_4',
          question: 'Examine the changes in the status of women from the Early to the Later Vedic Age.',
          answer: 'The contrast between women\'s status in the Early and Later Vedic periods represents one of the most significant social reversals in Indian history, reflecting the broader transformation from a mobile pastoralist society to a settled, caste-stratified agricultural one.\n\nIn the Early Vedic Age, women enjoyed high status and considerable freedom. They could study the Vedas and participate in religious ceremonies alongside their husbands. Some women composed Vedic hymns—Gargi (who debated publicly with Yajnavalkya) and Maitreyi (who chose spiritual knowledge over wealth) being the most celebrated. Women could attend public assemblies (Sabha and Samiti). Child marriage was unknown, and widow remarriage was permitted through the niyoga system.\n\nIn the Later Vedic Age (c. 1000–600 BCE), women\'s status deteriorated significantly. Child marriages became common. Women were increasingly excluded from Vedic education. Widow remarriage was discouraged. Women lost access to public assemblies. The Shatapatha Brahmana placed women in the same category as Shudras and gaming dice—objects of moral concern. Arranged marriages became the norm.\n\nThis decline is explained by: the rigidification of the varna system restricting women\'s access to knowledge; the emphasis on ritual purity making women\'s bodies a source of pollution during menstruation; and the consolidation of patriarchal agrarian societies in which women\'s property rights and public roles were curtailed.\n\nThis historical reversal had long-lasting consequences, contributing to the social inequalities that later reformers like Raja Ram Mohan Roy, Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar, and Jyotirao Phule sought to correct in the 19th century.',
