          id: '101_1_8_5',
          question: 'Describe the main features of Upanishadic philosophy.',
          answer: 'The Upanishads (c. 800–500 BCE), also called Vedanta (end of the Vedas), represent a philosophical revolution within the Vedic tradition—shifting focus from external ritual performance to inner spiritual inquiry. Over 200 Upanishads exist, of which 13 are considered principal.\n\nThe central doctrine is the identity of Atman (individual soul) and Brahman (universal spirit). The Upanishads teach that the apparent multiplicity of individual selves is an illusion (maya); ultimately, all existence is one undivided spiritual reality. This is expressed in the Mahavakyas (great sayings): "Aham Brahmasmi" (I am Brahman), "Tat tvam asi" (You are that), and "Prajnanam Brahma" (Consciousness is Brahman).\n\nThe Upanishads developed the concepts of karma (the law of action and consequences) and samsara (the cycle of birth, death, and rebirth). Liberation (moksha) is achieved through true knowledge (jnana) of Atman-Brahman identity, breaking the cycle of rebirth.\n\nThis philosophy was a reaction against the excessive ritualism of the Brahmana period. The Upanishadic teachers—Yajnavalkya, Uddalaka Aruni, Gargi—argued that no amount of ritual could secure true liberation; only spiritual knowledge could free the soul.\n\nUpanishadic philosophy had profound consequences: it inspired Shankaracharya\'s Advaita Vedanta (8th century CE); influenced Buddhism and Jainism in their questioning of ritual; contributed to the Bhakti movement\'s emphasis on inner devotion; and attracted Western philosophers like Schopenhauer and Emerson. The Upanishads remain among humanity\'s deepest philosophical contributions.',
