          answer: 'The Later Vedic Age (c. 1000–600 BCE) witnessed a significant transformation in political organization, as small tribal units of the Early Vedic period evolved into larger, more centralized kingdoms.\n\nThe position of the Raja (king) grew in power and prestige. Unlike the Early Vedic chief dependent on tribal consent, the Later Vedic king commanded military forces and a growing administrative apparatus. Royal power was reinforced by elaborate ceremonies: the Rajasuya (royal consecration) invested the king with divine authority; the Vajapeya (chariot race) renewed royal power; and the Ashvamedha (horse sacrifice) established the king\'s claim to territorial sovereignty—a horse was allowed to roam freely for a year, and the king claimed all land through which it passed.\n\nThe tribal assemblies (Sabha and Samiti) that had earlier checked the chief\'s power gradually lost influence, becoming advisory bodies dominated by Brahmin priests rather than open participatory assemblies.\n\nThe administrative structure became more elaborate. The king was assisted by ratnins: the Purohita (chief priest), Senani (commander), and others. The Gramani (village headman) administered local units.\n\nTerritory replaced kinship as the primary organizing principle of the state. The concept of Rashtra (kingdom) emerged—a geographically defined political unit rather than a tribal following.\n\nThis political evolution set the stage for the Mahajanapada period (c. 600–300 BCE) and the eventual rise of the Mauryan Empire, which unified the Indian subcontinent for the first time under a centralized administration.',
