          answer: 'The Vedic Age (c. 1500–600 BCE) is known primarily through literary sources—the Vedic texts—supplemented by archaeological evidence. Understanding these sources and their nature is fundamental to Vedic historiography.\n\nThe four Vedas are the primary sources. The Rigveda (1,028 hymns in 10 Mandalas) is the oldest and most important—its hymns reveal Early Vedic society, geography, religious practices, and values. The Samaveda and Yajurveda reflect Later Vedic religious practice. The Atharvaveda contains charms, spells, and folk religion, giving insight into popular belief.\n\nThe Brahmanas are prose texts attached to the Vedas, explaining sacrificial rituals. The Shatapatha Brahmana is particularly important for social history—it records changing gender norms, the spread of Aryan settlement into the Gangetic plains, and iron technology\'s agricultural impact. The Aranyakas (forest texts) bridge the Brahmanas and Upanishads philosophically.\n\nArchaeological sources supplement the literary record. The Painted Grey Ware (PGW) culture (c. 1200–600 BCE) found in the Upper Gangetic Doab corresponds geographically and chronologically to the Later Vedic Age. Iron tools and weapons of this period confirm the iron age transformation described in Vedic texts. Settlements at Hastinapura and Kausambi match Vedic geographical references.\n\nThe main limitation of Vedic sources is that they were composed by the priestly elite and reflect Brahminical perspectives. They tell us much about elite religious and social norms but less about commoner life and non-Aryan populations. Critical reading of both literary and archaeological sources is essential for reconstructing Vedic history.',
