          question: 'Describe the administrative system of the Delhi Sultanate.',
          answer: 'The Delhi Sultanate (1206–1526) developed a centralized administrative system blending Persian-Islamic governance with Indian practices. Understanding this system is key to comprehending medieval Indian governance.\n\nAt the apex was the Sultan with supreme executive, judicial, and military authority. Theoretically subordinate to the Caliph, Delhi Sultans ruled independently. The Sultan was assisted by a council of ministers, of which the Wazir (Prime Minister) was most important—managing the treasury and overall administration.\n\nThe empire was divided into provinces through the iqta system. Military officers (muqtis/iqtadars) were assigned land from which they collected revenue to maintain troops for the Sultan. This system prevented the development of hereditary feudal lords, as assignments could be rotated. The iqtadar was responsible for maintaining order, collecting taxes, and providing military service.\n\nMajor administrative departments included: Diwan-i-Wazarat (finance); Diwan-i-Arz (military); Diwan-i-Risalat (correspondence); and Diwan-i-Insha (royal orders). Alauddin Khalji (r. 1296–1316) strengthened centralization dramatically—establishing market price controls, building an intelligence network, and maintaining a large standing army directly paid from the treasury.\n\nAt the local level, the pargana (administrative subdivision) was governed by the shiqdar (law and order) and the munsif (revenue), while the village retained traditional self-governance under local headmen.\n\nThe Delhi Sultanate\'s administrative system established Persian-Islamic governance traditions in India—traditions that the Mughals later adapted and perfected into one of history\'s most sophisticated imperial systems.',
