          id: '101_3_8_3',
          question: 'Examine the main features of Mughal painting and its contribution to Indian art.',
          answer: 'Mughal painting emerged as one of the great artistic traditions of the early modern world, blending Persian, Central Asian, and Indian stylistic elements into a distinctive and highly refined idiom.\n\nThe tradition was established by Humayun, who brought two Persian masters—Mir Sayyid Ali and Abd al-Samad—from the Safavid court. Under Akbar, the imperial atelier expanded dramatically with over 100 painters, both Hindu and Muslim. The Hamzanama (14 volumes, 1,400 large paintings illustrating tales of Amir Hamza) is the greatest achievement of this period. The Akbarnama and illustrations of the Mahabharata and Ramayana show Akbar\'s multicultural patronage.\n\nJahangir was the greatest Mughal connoisseur of painting. He patronized a more refined, individualistic style—portraits and studies of natural history (birds, flowers, insects) of extraordinary accuracy. Ustad Mansur\'s bird paintings and Abu\'l Hasan\'s portraits achieved a delicacy unmatched in Islamic art. European pictorial conventions (shading, perspective, portraiture) were absorbed and adapted under Jahangir.\n\nShah Jahan\'s reign saw increasing formality and refinement, with court scenes and architectural portraits. After Aurangzeb restricted court painting on religious grounds, Mughal artists dispersed to regional courts (Rajput, Deccani, Pahari), spreading stylistic influences across India.\n\nMughal painting introduced naturalistic portraiture, landscape backgrounds, and scientific accuracy in natural history illustration—innovations that enriched both Indian and world art history. Its influence on Rajput, Deccan, and Pahari painting schools continued for two centuries after the Mughal court\'s decline.',
