          id: '101_3_8_5',
          question: 'Describe the Sufi movement and its contribution to Indo-Islamic culture.',
          answer: 'Sufism—the mystical tradition within Islam that seeks direct personal experience of God through love, devotion, and spiritual discipline—played a central role in shaping Indo-Islamic culture and facilitating Hindu-Muslim cultural synthesis.\n\nSufi teachers (pirs or shaykhs) first came to India with the Muslim invasions of the 12th century. They established silsilahs (orders), each with a distinctive spiritual lineage. The four most important Sufi orders in India were: the Chishti, Suhrawardi, Qadiri, and Naqshbandi orders.\n\nThe Chishti order was the most influential. Chishti masters like Khwaja Moinuddin Chishti at Ajmer, and Nizamuddin Auliya (1236–1325) in Delhi attracted enormous followings from both Muslims and Hindus. The Chishti emphasis on love (ishq), devotional music (sama), and service to the poor made it particularly accessible to Indians. Khwaja Moinuddin Chishti\'s dargah at Ajmer remains one of the most visited Muslim shrines in the world, pilgrimage to which by both Muslims and Hindus is a powerful symbol of syncretic religious culture.\n\nSufi music and poetry—including qawwali (devotional music) and Persian-Urdu ghazals—made lasting contributions to Indian classical music. The Bhakti-Sufi dialogue enriched both traditions, with similar emphases on personal devotion, rejection of ritual formalism, and divine love.\n\nAmeer Khusrau, the 14th-century poet-musician and disciple of Nizamuddin Auliya, is credited with founding the khayal form of classical music—personifying the creative synthesis that the Sufi tradition enabled.',
