          id: '101_3_8_6',
          question: 'Examine the revenue system of the Mughal Empire under Akbar.',
          answer: 'The Mughal revenue system under Akbar, particularly the Dahsala system developed by his finance minister Todar Mal, was one of the most sophisticated land revenue systems in pre-modern world history—providing the fiscal foundation for the empire\'s military and administrative power.\n\nPre-Akbar revenue collection was irregular and often oppressive. The early Mughals used the hasb-ul-hukm (royal order) system, which set revenue demands arbitrarily. Sher Shah Suri (1540–1545) had earlier introduced important improvements—measuring land, classifying it by crop, and setting standard rates—but these were not consistently implemented.\n\nTodar Mal\'s Dahsala (Ten-Year) system, implemented from 1580, involved: surveying and measuring agricultural land using standard units; classifying it into four categories (polaj—regularly cultivated, parati—fallow, chachar—uncultivated for 3–4 years, banjar—long-term waste); averaging production over 10 years; and fixing cash assessments at one-third of the average produce in cash.\n\nThe system was a major advance because: it provided fiscal predictability (the state knew what revenue to expect); rewarded agricultural improvement (improvements in productivity benefited the cultivator for the ten-year period before re-assessment); protected cultivators from arbitrary year-to-year fluctuations; and created detailed land records that served as the basis for governance.\n\nLandlords (zamindars) served as intermediaries between the state and cultivators, collecting revenue and retaining a commission (nankar). The cultivator (raiyat) enjoyed security of tenure under regular assessment.\n\nThis system became the model for subsequent Indian revenue settlements, including the British East India Company\'s early experiments and elements of the Ryotwari and Zamindari systems—making Akbar\'s revenue administration a milestone in Indian fiscal history.',
