          id: '101_4_8_1',
          question: 'Examine the role of Raja Ram Mohan Roy in Indian social reform.',
          answer: 'Raja Ram Mohan Roy (1772–1833), often called the "Father of Modern India," made extraordinary contributions to social reform, religious renaissance, and women\'s rights in early 19th-century India.\n\nRoy founded the Brahmo Sabha (1828, later Brahmo Samaj) in Calcutta as a monotheistic reform association rejecting polytheism, idol worship, and superstition. Drawing on rational analysis of the Upanishads, he argued original Hinduism was monotheistic and rational—distorted over time by priestly accretions. The Brahmo Samaj attracted the educated Bengali elite and became the intellectual center of the Bengal Renaissance.\n\nRoy\'s most celebrated achievement was his campaign against sati (widow immolation). Over many years he documented cases, organized public opinion, submitted petitions to the colonial government, and lobbied persistently. His efforts directly led to the Bengal Sati Regulation Act (1829) under Lord Bentinck. He then traveled to England and argued before the Privy Council to prevent the Act\'s repeal—the first Indian to lobby the British Parliament on social issues.\n\nIn education, Roy advocated Western scientific education. When the government proposed establishing a Sanskrit college, he famously argued for "a liberal and enlightened system of education" including mathematics, natural philosophy, chemistry, and anatomy. His founding of Hindu College (1817) advanced Western education in Calcutta.\n\nRoy also campaigned against polygamy, for widow remarriage, and for freedom of the press. His intellectual synthesis of Indian and Western thought—using Western rationalism to critique Hindu orthodoxy while using Hindu scripture to resist cultural imperialism—set the template for Indian modernism and influenced every subsequent reform movement.',
