          id: '101_4_8_2',
          question: 'Describe the contributions of Dayananda Saraswati and the Arya Samaj.',
          answer: 'Swami Dayananda Saraswati (1824–1883), a Sanskrit scholar from Gujarat, founded the Arya Samaj in 1875 at Bombay—one of the most dynamic Hindu reform organizations of the 19th century.\n\nDayananda\'s central thesis, expressed in Satyartha Prakash (Light of Truth, 1875), was "Back to the Vedas"—the original four Vedas represented pure, rational religion; later accretions of idolatry, polytheism, and caste discrimination had corrupted it. He accepted only the Vedas as infallible scripture, rejecting the Puranas, temple worship, image worship, and much of popular Hinduism.\n\nOn social issues the Arya Samaj was progressive. Dayananda condemned child marriage, the ban on widow remarriage, the untouchability system, and the exclusion of women from Vedic education. His ten principles (Dasa Niyamas) included commitment to truth, universal brotherhood, and social service.\n\nThe Shuddhi (purification) movement, developed by the Arya Samaj, was designed to reconvert Hindus who had converted to Christianity or Islam. This was controversial but significant for Hindu social consolidation.\n\nIn education, the Arya Samaj established the DAV (Dayananda Anglo-Vedic) school system across Punjab and northwestern India—combining Vedic studies with Western science. The DAV schools and colleges became important educational institutions that remain active today.\n\nThe Arya Samaj\'s assertive Hindu reformism, Western-style organization, and emphasis on Vedic rationalism made it particularly influential in Punjab, where it shaped 20th-century religious and social politics.',
