          id: '101_4_8_3',
          question: 'Analyze the Aligarh Movement and its significance for Muslim education.',
          answer: 'The Aligarh Movement, led by Sir Syed Ahmad Khan (1817–1898), was the most important Muslim reform and educational initiative of 19th-century India—seeking to reconcile Islamic faith with Western modernity and to bring Muslims into the mainstream of colonial education.\n\nSyed Ahmad Khan was deeply disturbed by two developments: the Muslims\' disproportionate suffering after the 1857 Rebellion (widely blamed on Muslim leadership) and Muslims\' backwardness in Western education compared to Hindus. He diagnosed the problem as Muslim refusal to engage with Western education and science, rooted in a perceived conflict between Western rationalism and Islamic faith.\n\nIntellectually, he argued through his journal Tehzeeb-ul-Akhlaq (Social Reformer) and Tafseer-ul-Quran that there was no fundamental conflict between the Quran and modern science—interpreting the Quran rationally to show its compatibility with scientific knowledge.\n\nEducationally, he founded the Mohammedan Anglo-Oriental College at Aligarh in 1875 (which became Aligarh Muslim University in 1920). The MAO College combined Western sciences and English-medium education with Islamic studies and a residential college structure modeled on Oxford and Cambridge. It trained Muslim professionals who could enter government service and the professions.\n\nPolitically, Syed Ahmad Khan discouraged Muslim participation in the Indian National Congress (1885), arguing democratic politics would be dominated by the Hindu majority. This stance contributed to the emergence of separate Muslim political consciousness, eventually leading to the formation of the Muslim League (1906) and influencing the course of Indian history.',
