          id: '101_4_8_4',
          question: 'Describe the role of Christian missionaries in India\'s education and social reform.',
          answer: 'Christian missionaries—Portuguese, then British and American Protestant—played a significant role in Indian society during the colonial period, contributing to education, printing, social reform, and cultural change.\n\nThe most immediate contribution was education. William Carey\'s Serampore Mission (1800) established a printing press for Indian languages and a college. Alexander Duff\'s General Assembly\'s Institution in Calcutta (1830) became an influential college combining Christian and Western education. Mission schools provided education in many areas where government schools were absent, particularly in tribal and low-caste communities.\n\nMissionaries made crucial contributions to Indian linguistics. Carey, Marshman, and Ward translated the Bible into multiple Indian languages, systematizing Bengali, Hindi, and other languages in the process—producing the first grammars and dictionaries of many Indian languages. This work had lasting intellectual significance beyond religion.\n\nIn social reform, missionaries were vocal opponents of sati, infanticide, and the caste system. Their public campaigns and advocacy before the colonial government supported reform-minded officials like Bentinck. They promoted female education at a time when most Indian traditions discouraged it—the many missionary girls\' schools were significant pioneers.\n\nThe conversion of lower-caste and outcaste communities to Christianity provided escape from caste discrimination—challenging the social order in ways that made high-caste Hindu reformers uncomfortable. The missionary critique of Hinduism provoked defensive responses from Hindu reformers, indirectly stimulating the Hindu reform movement itself.',
