          id: '101_4_8_5',
          question: 'Examine the contribution of Swami Vivekananda to Indian culture and religion.',
          answer: 'Swami Vivekananda (1863–1902), disciple of Sri Ramakrishna and founder of the Ramakrishna Mission, was the most globally influential Indian spiritual teacher of the 19th century—combining Vedantic philosophy with social activism and presenting Hinduism to the world as a rational, universal religion.\n\nVivekananda\'s landmark speech at the Parliament of the World\'s Religions in Chicago (1893) announced India\'s spiritual renaissance to a Western audience. Beginning with "Sisters and Brothers of America," he presented Hinduism not as a heathen religion but as a universal philosophy of Advaita Vedanta—arguing all paths lead to the same divine truth, making Hinduism the most tolerant of world religions. His Chicago speech made him internationally famous and attracted Western interest in Vedanta.\n\nBack in India, Vivekananda challenged Hindu society to recognize that spirituality without social service was incomplete. His famous dictum "Daridra Narayan" (God in the poor) asserted that serving the poor is serving God—transforming the Ramakrishna Mission into one of India\'s most effective charitable organizations, running hospitals, schools, and disaster relief.\n\nVivekananda was also a nationalist—arguing India\'s spiritual heritage was the country\'s greatest asset. His lectures inspired a generation of nationalists, including Bal Gangadhar Tilak and Subhas Chandra Bose.\n\nThe Ramakrishna Mission (founded 1897) continues as a major spiritual and philanthropic organization in India and internationally, embodying the synthesis of Vedantic philosophy and social service that was Vivekananda\'s enduring legacy.',
