          id: '101_4_8_6',
          question: 'Analyze the social reform movements in India and their impact on women.',
          answer: 'The 19th-century social reform movements addressed issues affecting women\'s status—particularly sati, widow remarriage, child marriage, female education, and purdah. These reforms collectively laid the foundation for significant changes in Indian women\'s lives.\n\nThe abolition of sati (1829) was the first major legislative achievement. Raja Ram Mohan Roy\'s persistent campaign persuaded Lord Bentinck to criminalize it. Though sati was declining in frequency, its legal abolition established that Indian "tradition" could be challenged through reform advocacy and legislation.\n\nWidow remarriage was championed by Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar (1820–1891). He mobilized scriptural evidence from the Parasara Smriti to argue widow remarriage was permitted by Hinduism—countering orthodoxy on its own terms. His petitioning led to the Hindu Widows\' Remarriage Act (1856).\n\nFemale education was promoted by Jyotirao Phule and his wife Savitribai Phule, who established the first school for girls from lower castes in Pune (1848). Vidyasagar established schools for girls in Bengal. D.K. Karve founded a widows\' home in Maharashtra. Pandita Ramabai established the Mukti Mission for women.\n\nThe Age of Consent controversy (1891) illuminated the conflict between reform and tradition: the colonial government raised the age of consent for girls from 10 to 12, opposed by Hindu conservatives. The debate accelerated nationalist consciousness even among reformers.\n\nThese reforms cumulatively improved women\'s legal status, expanded educational access, and created social space for the women\'s movement that would demand political rights in the 20th century and ultimately contributed to the constitutional guarantees of gender equality in independent India.',
